## AI app factory

This repository is worked on through the AI app factory. Its engine is installed outside the repository; everything it keeps here is under `.factory/`: governance settings in `.factory/config.json` (models, gates, review, commands), the application's topology in `.factory/app.json` (services, test stack ports, URLs, readiness and publish overrides), agent addenda, lessons, architecture policy, test plans and test stack scenarios. Run it as `factory <command>`; when `factory` is not on your PATH, use `.factory/bin/factory <command>`.

- To run stories through the whole pipeline: `factory implement run <KEY>...` (`--parallel N` runs several at once). It starts the implementer in each story's own worktree and branch; you don't need the steps below.
- When you start implementing a story by hand, run `factory start <KEY>`.
- When your line of work for that story is complete, run `factory done` before you finish. The Stop hook then runs the architecture review. If it blocks with findings, fix them and finish again; tests are written automatically after approval.
- Stories, epics, spikes and mockups are tickets on the story board, cached in `.factory/state/board/<KEY>/`. Ignore that cache except for the story handed to the pipeline (`factory start <KEY>` or `factory implement run`). A PreToolUse hook enforces this.
- Do not edit `.factory/` or the files listed in `.factory/manifest.json` while implementing a story: they belong to the factory, not to the application. The exceptions are the application's topology in `.factory/app.json` (a story that adds a service registers it there) and the test stack it generates (`factory teststack publish`). Change generated files through their source and `factory render`. `factory check` fails when a generated file was edited by hand.
- Tests the pipeline writes live in the application's own test tree and are tagged with their story; treat them like any other test.

## graphify

This project has a knowledge graph at `graphify-out/` (built locally; run `factory graphify-build` when `graphify-out/graph.json` is missing).

- Run `graphify query "<question>"` before your first grep, search or source-file read for a codebase question, and put this rule in every subagent prompt that explores code. Read raw files only for lines the graph or an earlier read already located.
- Use `graphify path "<A>" "<B>"` for relationships and `graphify explain "<concept>"` for focused concepts.
- After modifying code, run `graphify update .` from the repository root to keep the graph current (no API cost). Always pass `.`: `graphify update <dir>` writes a second graph into that folder.

## Code intelligence (LSP)

The MCP server `lsp` exposes this repository's language servers. Use it for precise work at code you already located:

- Before changing existing code, confirm it: `lsp_goto_definition` and `lsp_hover` for the real definition and signature, `lsp_find_references` for every caller before you change a signature or behavior.
- After every edit, run `lsp_diagnostics` on the edited files.
- Rename with `lsp_rename` (`dry_run: true` first) instead of search and replace.
- Paths are absolute and inside your own checkout; line and column are 1-indexed. When a call answers "language server unavailable", do not retry in a loop: search instead.

## Feature videos (definition of done)

Every e2e test is recorded on video (`video` in the Playwright config). By default only failed tests keep theirs (`artifacts/e2e-videos/<project>/<spec>--<test>.failed.webm`, plus keyframes for agents); set `E2E_KEEP_VIDEOS=1` to keep passing tests' videos too. When you implement or change anything a user can see or do in the browser, you are not done until a passing e2e test covers the flow and you have its video. Follow the factory's `feature-demo-video` skill (`skills/feature-demo-video/SKILL.md` in the factory install; `factory version --path` prints where that is):

1. Add or extend an e2e spec for the flow on the test stack (`e2e/teststack/<feature>.spec.ts`), narrated with the `demo` fixture from `e2e/demo.ts`. Specs import `test` from `e2e/fixtures.ts` (or `e2e/demo.ts`), never from `@playwright/test`, so contexts opened with `browser.newContext()` are recorded too.
2. Run it: `factory teststack up`, then `E2E_KEEP_VIDEOS=1 npx playwright test -c playwright.teststack.config.ts <feature>` (PowerShell: `$env:E2E_KEEP_VIDEOS=1` first). It must pass.
3. Embed the resulting `artifacts/e2e-videos/**/*.webm` (absolute path) in your final summary, and describe what it shows in the PR description. Never commit the videos.

Skip this only for backend-only, docs-only or no-behavior-change work, and say so in your summary.
